## 1. Introduction
Atmospheric pollution caused by particulate matters (PMs) constitutes a huge challenge globally, posing serious threats to personal health [1-4]. PMs are composed of nitrate, sulfate, silicate, carbon, ammonium, etc., which can be viewed as a complex aerosol. Commonly, PMs are categorized by theirs sizes, as $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ refers to particles with a size below $2.5~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , and its negative effects increase with decreasing size [5,6]. Numerous efforts have been dedicated to the development of high-efficiency air filters for air quality control [7-10]. Diverse synthetic/natural polymers including polypropylene, polyetherimide, polyurethane, cellulose, lignin, and chitosan have been used as the base media for air filters [11- 18]. Fibrous air filters are produced by randomly stacking fibrils and they exhibit high porosity and low density [6,19,20]. Although fibrous filtering systems have advanced rapidly, it remains to be a challenge to manufacture filtration materials with high efficiency, low cost, high air flux, low-pressure drop, and good reusability [8,21].
Recently, environmentally benign wood materials fabricated from top-down technology have been proven to be an appealing alternative for filters because of their anisotropic architecture and interconnected network [22-24]. They have found wide applications including thermal insulating, solar desalination, pressure sensing and wastewater purification [25-27]. For example, Chen et al. coated Pd nanoparticles on the three-dimensional wood filter and used them for catalytic flow degradation of methylene blue. The wastewater treatment rate of the composite filter could reach $1\times10^{5}\mathrm{L}\mathrm{m}^{-2}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with a removal efficiency of $99.8\%$ [28]. In addition, top-down wood nanotechnology enables the preparation of highly compressible aerogel/sponge by partially or fully removing lignin/hemicellulose from the wood substrate. Guan et al. fabricated wood aerogels with a spring-like lamellar structure and the aerogel possesses an oil adsorption capacity of $41\ \mathrm{g\g^{-1}}$ . It can be directly used as a filter for oil/water separation and the used filter can be easily regenerated through squeezing [29]. Apart from water purification [30,31], wood-based filters hold great potential for gas capture and PM removal. Okubayashi et al. impregnated polyethylenimine into the mesoporous wood matrix and the modified wood foam showed a ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ adsorption capacity of $3.0\mathrm{mmol}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [32]. Prior studies have demonstrated that wood is an excellent substrate/support for metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) deposition and growth. MOFs are a kind of organic–inorganic hybrid materials with intramolecular pores formed by self-assembly of organic ligands and metal ions or clusters through coordination bonds, which have proved huge potential in the field of gas treatment [33,34]. The abundant surface hydroxyl groups of cellulose in wood matrix are beneficial for the hybridization of MOFs. Wang et al. integrated different kinds of MOFs into the deep-eutecticsolvent treated wood. Due to the suitable surface charge property and sophisticated hierarchical channels, the MOF functionalized filter possessed desirable PM removal capability [35]. However, a trade-off constraint exists between the efficiency and pressure drop, necessitating the design and preparation of high-efficiency filters.
Herein, with delignified wood aerogels as the substrate, ZIF-L and UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ were introduced into the matrix through in situ growth process. ZIF-L is selected due to its leaf-like morphology and positively charged surface, whereas UiO- ${\cdot}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is chosen based on its large surface area and abundant $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ groups. The morphology and pore property of composite aerogels can be regulated by the MOF deposition, and there is a good interface affinity between wood and the MOF layer. Air filtration tests showed that the integration of MOFs significantly boosted the PM removal efficiency, and the pressure drop just increased slightly upon MOF loading. The PM removal mechanisms in the wood-based filters are discussed in detail. Moreover, the MOF coated aerogels are facile to be regenerated and have superior reusability and durability.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Natural balsa wood (Ochroma pyramidale) was provided by the Jushi Decoration Materials Company (Shanghai, China). The wood cuboids were cut into sheets with a dimension of $40\times40\times5~\mathrm{mm}^{3}$ (tangential $\times$ radial $\times$ axial). Mosquito coil incense was bought from Zhongshan Lanju Daily Chemical Industry Company. Sodium chlorite $(\mathsf{N a C l O}_{2}$ , $80.0\%$ , 2- methylimidazole $(98.0\%)$ , zirconium chloride $(\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4},98.0\%)$ and 2- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ -terephthalic acid $(98.0\%)$ were obtained from Aladdin Industrial Company. Acetic acid ( $\mathrm{\cdot}C\mathrm{H}_{3}C00\mathrm{H}$ , $99.5\%$ , sodium hydroxide (NaOH, $\geq97.0\%$ , sodium sulfite $(\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{S O}_{3},\mathsf{\Omega}_{2}\mathsf{98}.0\%)$ , ethanol $:\mathrm{CH}_{3^{-}}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ , $97.0\%$ ) and N, $N\mathrm{.}$ -dimethyl formamide (DMF, $99.8\%$ ) were provided by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Company. Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $\geq99.0\%$ was bought from the Nanjing Chemical Reagent Company.
## 2.2. Preparation of wood aerogels
The wood sheets were ultrasonically rinsed with ethanol and dried at $80^{\circ}C$ for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ before the removal of lignin and hemicellulose by using ${\mathsf{N a C l O}}_{2}$ and NaOH solution, respectively. The wood sheets (ca. $_{\mathrm{~4~g,~5~}}$ pieces) were submerged in ${\mathsf{N a C l O}}_{2}$ solution $1000\ \mathrm{mL}$ , $12.5\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ with a $\mathsf{p H}$ of 4.6 (adjusted by $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ , $99.5\%$ . The mixed suspension was then transferred into an oven $(80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ and kept for another $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ The wood sample was collected and washed with deionized water, then they were immersed into a solution containing $\mathsf{N a O H}$ $(2.5\mathrm{\mol\L^{-1}},$ ) and ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf S}{\bf O}_{3}$ $\mathrm{\cdot}0.4\mathrm{\mol}$ ${\mathrm{L}}^{-1}.$ ), and heated to $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After washing with deionized water, the wood sample was freeze-dried $(-52^{\circ}\mathsf{C},24\mathrm{h})$ to acquire wood aerogels. The removal efficiencies of lignin and hemicellulose were evaluated by the National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL) method [27].
## 2.3. Preparation of MOF decorated wood aerogels
The MOFs (i.e. ZIF-L and $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H_{2}}$ were integrated into wood aerogels in a facile way. Typically, $_{0.595\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ was added to $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water under stirring, and wood aerogels (one piece) were put into the above solution. Afterward, $1.313\mathrm{~g~}$ of 2-methylimidazole was dispersed in another $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water to obtain a uniform solution. This solution was mixed with the wood aerogel-containing suspension under stirring. After $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , the modified wood aerogels were collected, washed with deionized water, and freeze-dried to obtain a ZIF-L@Wood filter.
For the synthesis of the UiO- $66\mathrm{-}\mathsf{N H}_{2}@\mathsf{W}00\mathsf{d}$ filter, wood aerogels (one piece) were put into DMF $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\mathrm{40\mL}}$ solution containing $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ $90.3\mathrm{mg})$ under stirring. After 12 h, 41.7 mL of DMF containing $2\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ -terephthalic acid $\mathrm{~:147~mg)}$ and acetic acid $(4.8\ \mathrm{g})$ was added to the above suspension under stirring. Following that, the mixed suspension was transferred into a Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave and heated to $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ The treated samples were washed with deionized water and freeze-dried. In addition, the ZIF-L and UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ particles synthesized in the suspension were collected by centrifugation and used as reference.
## 2.4. Characterizations
## 2.5. Air purification tests
The model PM particles were produced by the combustion of mosquito coil incense and the removal efficiencies were estimated by a homemade setup (Fig. S1, supporting information). An electric fan was used to control the airflow speed. The PM concentration was evaluated by an air detector (VSON WP6919S, China) and the pressure drop was recorded by a pressure detector (SMART SENSOR AS510, China). The used wood filter can be regenerated by washing with a mixed solution containing $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and $50\ \mathrm{\mL}$ of water. In addition, all air filtration tests were repeated three times.
The PM removal efficiency $(\upeta)$ and the quality factor $(\mathsf{Q}_{\mathsf{F}})$ were determined via Eqs. (1) and (2):
$$
\eta=\frac{C_{0}-C_{1}}{C_{0}}\times100\%
$$
$$
\mathsf Q_{\mathtt{F}}=\frac{-1\mathsf n(1-\eta)}{\Delta\mathsf P}
$$
where $\mathbf{c}_{\mathrm{o}}\left(\upmu\mathrm{g}.\mathrm{m}^{-3}\right)$ and $\mathbf{c}_{1}(\upmu\mathbf{g}.\mathbf{m}^{-3})$ are the pollutant concentrations before and after filtration, respectively and $\Delta\mathsf{P}$ indicates the pressure drop.
## 3. Results and discussions
3.1. Fabrication and characterization of air filters.
Natural balsa wood possesses partially aligned channels, in which cellulose fibrils are wrapped by molecularly mixed lignin and hemicellulose. Fig. 1 illustrates the immobilization process of ZIF-L and $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ . To increase the wood substrate porosity and promote the cellulose chain availability, alkaline treatment is utilized to remove lignin and hemicellulose. The delignification process removes $100\%$ of lignin and $86.4\%$ of hemicellulose, and the wood surface charge changes from $^{-12}$ to $-22{\mathrm{~mV}}$ (Fig. S2, supporting information). Analyzed by FT-IR spectra, the characteristic peaks at 1735 (stretching vibration of hemicellulose acetyl groups) and $1505~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (stretching vibration of lignin aromatic rings) are weakened upon delignification (Fig. S3, supporting information) [36,37]. This suggests the successful removal of lignin and hemicellulose. The elimination of hydrophobic lignin and a more negative Zeta potential could promote the impregnation and attachment of metal ions. The cellulose hydroxyl groups in delignified wood matrix are highly exposed and provide abundant anchor sites for $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ or ${\cal Z}\mathrm{r}^{4+}$ ions. The addition of corresponding organic ligands enables the generation of MOF crystals in wood outer surface and inner channels.
As displayed in Fig. 2a, FT-IR spectra are recorded to explore the possible group crosslinkage within the composite aerogels. Delignified wood aerogels present typical cellulose absorption peaks at 3430, 1622 and $1018~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which are in line with the peaks of cellulose materials. The ZIF-L exhibits peaks at around 1584, 1200 and $423~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , belonging to the stretching vibrations of the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{N}$ group, bending vibration of imidazole ring and stretching vibration of $Z\mathrm{n-N}$ linkages, respectively [38]. While UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ bears an obvious peak at $669~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which is originated from the $Z\mathrm{r}–0$ stretching vibration [39]. The peaks at 1580, 1400, and $1260~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ are assigned to the carboxyl group, the $c{-}0$ bond in the benzene ring and the $\mathsf{C-N}$ bonds, respectively. After deposition of ZIF-L and $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ , the signals of the MOFs are well inherited by the composite filters. XRD measurements are performed to investigate the crystalline patterns of composites (Fig. 2b). The peaks appeared at $7.3^{\circ}$ $11.1^{\circ}$ , $16.9^{\circ}$ and $18.0^{\circ}$ are indexed to (200), (112), (023), and (420) planes of ZIF-L [38]. And the peaks at $7.4^{\circ},8.5^{\circ},12.0^{\circ},25.7^{\circ}$ and $33.1^{\circ}$ are attributed to (111), (002), (022), (224) and (137) planes of UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ [39]. The MOF peaks are well preserved in the ZIF-L@Wood and UiO$66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathrm{W}00\mathrm{d}$ further evidencing the successful deposition of MOFs. Wood aerogel and MOF@Wood samples were characterized by XPS to confirm the interaction between MOF and the wood (Fig. S4, supporting information). The MOF crystals are attached tightly to the highly available cellulose surface groups.
TG curves were performed to investigate the MOF loadings in composite filters. As displayed in Fig. 3a, the weight loss in TG curves below $250^{\circ}\mathsf C$ is ascribed to the removal of adsorbed moisture. The sharp weight loss occurred around $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is originated from the decomposition of cellulose. Determined from the different weight method, the ZIF-L and UiO-66- $\left.\mathsf{N H}_{2}\right.$ content in composite filters are estimated to be $36~\mathrm{wt\%}$ and 29 wt%. To explore the pore structure of MOF@Wood samples, $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms were recorded in Fig. 3b. Bare wood exhibits a low BET surface area of $0.9~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ because it is dominated by macrosized channels (Fig. S5, supporting information). As a comparison, delignified wood possesses a surface area of $2.0\mathrm{~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ . This increase is ascribed to the lignin and hemicellulose removal, which creates nanopores and improves the exposure of pits and ray cells.
For MOF powders, UiO- $66\substack{-\mathsf{N H}_{2}}$ has a high specific surface area of $732{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}}{\ensuremath{\mathrm{~g}}^{-1}}$ , whereas ZIF-L presents a specific surface area of $21~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [25]. After the deposition of MOF particles in delignified wood, UiO-66 ${\mathsf{-N H}}_{2}@{\mathsf{W}}{\mathsf{o o d}}$ and ZIF- $\operatorname{L}@$ Wood show specific surface areas of 46 and $6\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Confirmed by pore size distribution (Fig. S6, supporting information), it is referred that hierarchical pores were constructed within the composite.
Fig. 4 displays the digital photos and SEM images of composite filters. The original wood sample is light-yellow in color, and it has aligned micrometer-sized channels along the tree growth direction (Fig. S7, supporting information), while the wood aerogels are white in color. In the cross-sectional image, it can be observed that the inner surface of wood aerogel channel is loose (Fig. 4a,). Upon the deposition of ZIF-L, the inner channel surface gets rougher. At a higher resolution, the randomly packed leaf-like ZIF-L layer can be identified. Verified by the EDX mapping of Zn element, ZIF-L is uniformly distributed (Fig. 4b). For the UiO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}@\mathsf{W o o d}$ filter, as shown in Fig. 4c, the $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ adheres tightly to the surface, giving the composite a high MOF-wood interface compatibility. Element mapping of $Z\mathrm{r}$ further proves the even distribution of MOF particles. There is a significant difference between the morphology of ZIF-L@Wood and UiO-66- $\cdot\N{\mathrm{H}}_{2}@ $ Wood, which might lead to different PM removal processes.
## 3.2. Air filtering tests
To assess the air-purification potential of as-prepared filters, the removal tests of $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ and $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ were conducted. The filter efficiencies and pressure drops of the resulting filters are summarized in Fig. 5. Wood aerogel filter exhibits relatively low filtration efficiencies, with $70.7\%$ for $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ and $75.0\%$ for $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ (Fig. 5a). This is because of the micrometer-sized channels and lack of functional agents. The pressure drop is estimated at $15\mathrm{Pa}$ The filtering ability of delignified wood is mainly derived from the porous wood matrix, which can intercept PM pollutants. After MOF deposition, the PM capture ability of the composite filters is greatly enhanced. ZIF-L decorated wood aerogels exhibited $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ and $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ removal efficiencies of $97.6\%$ and $99.5\%$ , while UiO-66- $\cdot\ensuremath{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ functionalized wood aerogels demonstrated $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ and $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ removal efficiencies of $96.4\%$ and $98.9\%$ . The MOF introduction also promotes the increase of pressure drops, the UiO-66 ${\left.-N H_{2}@{\mathsf{W}00}\mathsf{d}}\right.$ filter displays a pressure drop of ca. $21\ \mathrm{Pa}$ and the ZIF-L@Wood filter possesses a pressure drop of ca. $25\ \mathsf{P a}$ . It reveals that MOF coating boosts the gas transmission resistance. As observed from SEM images, ZIF-L modified filter has a more disordered structure than that of the UiO- $66\substack{-\Delta\mathsf{N}}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ modified filter, leading to a more tortuous gas pathway and higher pressure drop.
By adjusting the wind velocity, the pressure drop can be finely tuned. As illustrated in Fig. 5b and 5c, when the wind velocity increases, the filter pressure drop also increases, and the filtration efficiencies of $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ and $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ decrease. Among the three filters, ZIF-L@Wood demonstrates a good tolerance toward pressure drop variation. The filtration efficiency of $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ decreases from $99.1\%$ to $97.6\%$ and $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ decreases from $99.8\%$ to $99.5\%$ by changing the wind velocity from 2 to $6\mathrm{~m~}s^{-1}$ . The removal efficiencies of ZIFL@Wood filter were checked at various initial PM concentrations.
When $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ concentration or $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ concentration is $100\sim1000~{\upmu\mathrm{g}}~\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ , the removal efficiencies almost keep steady (Fig. 5d). When the wind velocity is $6\mathrm{m}s^{-1}$ with the PM concentration of $500\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{m}^{-3}$ , the calculated quality factors of ZIF-L@Wood for $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ and $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ are $0.15\ \mathrm{Pa}^{-1}$ and $0.21~\mathsf{P a}^{-1}$ , respectively, and the quality factors almost remained unchanged with the variation of PM concentrations (Fig. 5d).
ZIF-L@Wood filter was used as the optimal sample and studied in PM removal for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ and it presents a stable performance (Fig. 6a). The filter proves a good stability and the filtration efficiency of $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ $\mathrm{\PM}_{10})$ keeps more than $97.6\%$ $(99.5\%)$ after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ with a filtration capacity of $1.7\mathrm{L}s^{-1}$ . After $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the removal efficiencies still remain at $96.4\%$ $\lceil\rceil\mathbb{M}_{2.5})$ and $99.2\%$ $\left(\mathrm{PM}_{10}\right)$ . The used filter can be regenerated and it has a good reusability. The removal efficiency remains unchanged after 5 cycles (Fig S8). In addition, the filter displayed a high resistance toward humidity and the $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ and $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ filtration efficiency just decreased slightly to $95.5\%$ and $98.0\%$ after $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ of testing in a humid environment $25^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ , $50\%$ RH), respectively. The possible PM removal pathway of MOF@Wood filter is proposed in Fig. 6b. Physical interception and electrostatic interaction play a combined role in trapping these particles [40]. The sufficient hydroxyl groups in fibrous filter matrix could serve as anchor sites for PMs. The 3D porous filter provides a random path for PM movement, where PM particles collide directly on the surface of cellulose. $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ with a larger diameter displays a higher possibility to be captured by the filter matrix via physical effects [41]. Electrostatic interactions are indispensable for trapping PM particles. The MOF@Wood filter can effectively remove PM by electrostatic interaction. In this work, both ZIF-L and $\mathsf{U I O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ are MOFs with positive surface charges [42,43]. At the same time, polar functional groups such as $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ on the surface of MOFs can also improve the capture ability of PM, so the UIO-66- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\ @$ @Wood filter has a good air filtration performance [44]. Meanwhile, the particle size of ZIF-L is large. When ZIF-L is loaded inside the pores of wood aerogels, the internal structure of the pores can be changed, and the intercepting area can be increased, which can increase the interception and impaction of PM. So that ZIF-L@Wood filter has a good filtration efficiency, $97.6\%$ for $\mathsf{P M}_{2.5}$ and $99.5\%$ for $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ . Such obtained removal efficiencies are comparable with cellulose or MOF-based filters [45-47].
## 4. Conclusions
In this work, by using in situ growth approach, an air filter with a hierarchically porous structure was fabricated by encapsulating MOF (i.e. ZIF-L and $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}.$ particles into delignified wood matrix. By integrating different MOFs into wood channels, the porosity, surface charge and morphology of the composite filter can be tuned. The MOF crystals adhere tightly to the exposed cellulose surface groups and are distributed uniformly. Upon the incorporation of MOFs, the composites are used as air filters and the existence of MOFs is beneficial for the PM capture. The introduction of two-dimensional ZIF-L could regulate the filter channel morphology and enhances the interception of PM. In addition, the presence of amine-functionalized $\mathsf{U i O-}66\mathsf{-N H}_{2}$ imparts the filter with polar group and increases the removal efficiency. The removal efficiencies of ZIF-L decorated wood filter for $\mathrm{PM}_{2.5}$ and $\mathrm{PM}_{10}$ could exceed $97.6\%$ , and $99.5\%$ , respectively. The removal mechanism is ascribed to the increased interception/impaction of PM with the positively charged ZIF-L layer and the hierarchically porous structure. The PM removal performance of such wood filter is superior to other $\mathsf{M O F@}$ synthetic polymer filters and nanocellulose filters. This work provides insights into the design and preparation of novel materials for indoor air quality protection from sustainable and abundant biomass.
